Short:        MIT TCP/IP stack: bsdsocket.library on NetX Duo
Author:       aminetxduo (see the archive for contact details)
Uploader:     aminetxduo (see the archive for contact details)
Type:         comm/net
Version:      @VERSION@
Architecture: m68k-amigaos >= 2.04
Requires:     68020 or better, 4 MB RAM, a SANA-II network driver
Distribution: Aminet
Replaces:     comm/net/AmiNetXDuo.lha

AmiNetXDuo is a TCP/IP stack for classic AmigaOS built on Eclipse ThreadX
NetX Duo.  It provides the bsdsocket.library API that Amiga networking
software already speaks, over the SANA-II driver interface that Amiga network
cards already implement, and it is MIT licensed throughout -- no 4.4BSD and
no GPL lineage anywhere in the tree.

Included:

  bsdsocket.library     the socket API, self-starting on first OpenLibrary()
  usergroup.library     the companion library ported Unix tools link against
  AddNetInterface       bring an interface up from its description file
  Online / Offline      take one interface up or down
  ShowNetStatus         is it up, and what address did it get
  ping, netstat, host   the usual diagnostics
  an Installer script   asks two questions and writes a working config

Configuration follows Roadshow's layout -- DEVS:NetInterfaces/<name>,
DEVS:Internet/routes, DEVS:Internet/name_resolution and the standard
/etc-style netdb files -- so existing documentation and habits carry over.

What is in it that no classic Amiga stack has had:

  * IPv6.  The dual stack build does ICMPv6, neighbour discovery, duplicate
    address detection, stateless autoconfiguration from router
    advertisements, and AF_INET6 sockets through bsdsocket.library.  As far
    as we can establish this is an Amiga first.  It is a build option, off by
    default, so the floor build stays small.
  * An MIT licence with no strings.

Requires a 68020 or better and AmigaOS 2.04 or newer; developed and tested
on 3.1.  You supply your card's SANA-II driver -- a2065.device,
ariadne.device, ariadne2.device, amiganet.device, xsurf.device,
xsurf100.device, cnet.device and the PCMCIA drivers are all offered by name
in the installer, and anything else can be typed in.

Status, honestly: it gets a DHCP lease, answers ARP, pings its gateway,
resolves DNS and moves TCP in both directions, and bsdsocket.library scores
125 of 142 on the bsdsocktest conformance suite.  It has not been run on real
hardware -- everything measured so far was on an emulated 68020 and 68030
under Kickstart 3.1.  It is not yet something to trust with your data.
